Say which view claims a tag.

viewWithTag: returns one object, and when that object is the wrong one the return value cannot say
why. The caller then sends a selector belonging to the class it expected and raises on a class it
never asked for, which names the receiver but not the search that produced it, and the tags of the
views that were passed over are exactly what is missing.

MoneyMoney's showPreferences: does

    [[[self popoverController] view] viewWithTag: 1]

and then sends setTitle: and setHasPriority: to the result. setHasPriority: belongs to MMButton, the
result was an MMHelpButton, and the raise is caught by the @try around action delivery, so
Preferences simply never appears and nothing is logged. With the trace the search is not a guess:

    CIDER_VIEWTAG visit NSView         tag=-1 want=1 subviews=8
    CIDER_VIEWTAG visit MMLabel        tag=4  want=1 subviews=0
    CIDER_VIEWTAG visit MMButton       tag=3  want=1 subviews=0
    CIDER_VIEWTAG visit MMHelpButton   tag=1  want=1 subviews=0

which EXONERATES the search and the tag decoding both. The help button really does carry tag 1 in
the decoded tree, its siblings carry 4, 3, 0 and 2, and the walk stops at the first match as it
should. That moves the question to the search root or to the class, and it took one run rather
than a rebuild per hypothesis.

Off by default. viewWithTag: is called during layout, and a per visit line is only wanted while a
wrong view is being hunted.

Task #254.

--- a/AppKit/NSView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSView.m
@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@
 #import <objc/message.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSViewController.h>
 #import <objc/runtime.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSApplication.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSClipView.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSColor.h>
@@ -1240,6 +1242,15 @@
 - viewWithTag: (NSInteger) tag {
     int i, count = [_subviews count];

+    /* WHICH VIEW CLAIMS THE TAG. The answer is one object and the search cannot say whether the
+     * others were wrong, absent or merely later in the array, and a caller that gets the wrong
+     * view raises on a selector belonging to the class it expected. Task #254. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_VIEWTAG") != NULL) {
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_VIEWTAG visit %-28s tag=%ld want=%ld subviews=%d\n",
+                object_getClassName(self), (long) _tag, (long) tag, count);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
+
     if (_tag == tag)
         return self;
